Explanation: The volatile variable running ensures that updates made by one thread (in stop()) are visible to the thread running the loop in start().volatile vs synchronizedvolatilesynchronizedProvides visibility of reads and writes to a variable across threads.Provides mutual exclusion and establishes visibility guarantees around the synchronized region.Does not use an intrinsic lock for accessing the variable.Uses an object's intrinsic monitor lock.Does not make compound operations such as count++ atomic.Can protect compound operations when all accesses to the shared state follow the same synchronization strategy.Applied to variables.Applied to methods or blocks.Does not prevent multiple threads from accessing the variable simultaneously.Allows only one thread at a time to execute code guarded by the same monitor.Suitable for simple state flags where atomic compound updates are not required.Suitable for critical sections that require coordinated access to shared mutable state.
